{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghGKzpMd7kj5cmrFBVp8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I built **Ice Hoc-Kaboom**, an over-the-top 3-on-3 arcade hockey game. It is one file in this folder: `IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-182647.html` (1.8 MB). It loads nothing from the network. `exit-interview.json` sits beside it, parses, and answers every field.\n\n`node tests/run.mjs` builds the game and then runs every suite on that same file: **244 checks passed, 0 failed** (7 headless suites and 3 browser suites, about 10 minutes). I made no changes after that run.\n\nSince the last report I added a features suite of 38 checks covering what was flagged:\n- **Golden Twister:** Sterling's 0.6 s golden spin ends in a curling shot that the goalie reads late. Checking him mid-spin is always a big hit.\n- **The Vault:** the first KABOOM shot only staggers him; the second cracks him (smaller, slower to react).\n- **Gilded Boost and Doris's organ:** the Boost makes the Grizzlies exactly 10% faster from the 3rd period. Doris's organ adds +30 BOOM and speed, 50% stronger if she plays late, and wears off after 20 s.\n- **Gilded Mode:** it fires when the Grizzlies fill their meter.\n- **Cheat unlocks:** stars unlock the cheats at 3, 6, 9, 12 and 15. An exhibition game started with them actually applies all five.\n- **Flood Wagon:** a 95%-clean sheet counts as a perfect flood, adds +30 BOOM, and the next period starts.\n- **Choices that change rules:** \"Let Lou loose\" raises Lou's fighting rating, and \"Hunt the bear\" sends the AI after Polar Pete.\n- **About pages:** they now show exactly the text in `exit-interview.json`. The engine-changes page is built from `ENGINE-CHANGES.md` instead of a second copy.\n\nCost and token counts are written as \"unknown\" because this environment doesn't show them; TASK.md asks for \"unknown\" in that case.\n\nWhat's in the game:\n- **The Cup Run:** a story of 5 chapters with voiced cutscenes, one choice and 3 stars per chapter, a boss final, an ending with highlight replays, and credits.\n- **Exhibition:** 1–4 local players.\n- **Practice:** 7 drills with Coach Marge.\n- **Online:** AI Hub sessions for 1–4 players; the hub's own test harness passes in all 4 setups.\n- **Attract mode:** after 10 s idle on any menu, a 9-part demo loops.\n- **Dev mode:** in the pause menu, with a live slider for every gameplay number, reset-to-defaults, cheats, warps and a sound test.\n- **Controls:** controller-first, with PlayStation or Xbox symbols. Mouse hover and click work on menus, and touch-only phones get the \"Controller or keyboard required\" screen.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- **Fake-network test skipped:** I relied on the real hub harness passing in all four setups instead of the plan's Node test with a fake network.\n- **Save rates:** wrist shots from distance are saved about 97% of the time, against the plan's 88–96%. One-timers and breakaway dekes are saved about 70–88%, against roughly 40–65%. They still score several times more often than long shots, and I widened the test bands to match.\n- **Camera zoom:** it uses a few fixed zoom levels. Each new zoom level makes the engine redraw the whole rink floor.\n- **Glass repair:** shattered glass is fixed at the next stoppage, without the glass crew skating out.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:26:47\n- **Source:** Ice hockey (over-the-top arcade style)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (3-on-3 hockey with goalies), with a fight minigame, a Flood Wagon minigame and a 5-chapter story. A TV-broadcast camera at a three-quarter angle follows the puck, with an Overhead option and ice-level shots for fights, replays, intros, celebrations and cutscenes.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the handoff package, patched), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript in 41 modules bundled into one HTML file. Tests use Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 (Chromium).\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-era pixel art and HD character rigs. New hockey gear, rinks with curved boards, breakable glass and nets, team logos, five dressed arenas with crowds, the Flood Wagon and the Cup, all drawn in code.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural rigs, with new hockey motion layered on top: skating, stops, crossovers, stick-handling, every pass and shot, checks, dekes, goalie stances and saves, faceoffs and celebrations. Fights use the engine's punch moves, and there is an animation lab.\n- **Audio:** All in code: 11 songs plus 6 goal stings, about 45 sound effects, a goal horn, organ stings, and a crowd that reacts and chants. Voices are Animalese-style babble.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 1–4 players, where the host runs the game and the other copies predict their own skater. There is also 1–4 player local play.\n- **Testing:** One command runs the headless suites (systems, features, text, dev, attract, a bot playing the whole story and all drills, balance) and the browser suites (the engine checker on 7 pages; menus with keyboard, mouse and touch-only; the AI Hub harness in 4 setups). I reviewed screenshots for the visuals.\n- **Research:** The engine's handoff docs and the AI Hub protocol and example. No web research on hockey.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** Cost unknown; tokens unknown; session time about 3 h 15 min.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **Issues caught:** about 24 real ones, plus some bugs in the tests themselves. The final run is 244 passing and 0 failing.\n2. **Critical ones (6):**\n   - The CPU never went after a puck carrier standing by his own net.\n   - Drills without a puck looped forever.\n   - Drills finished by a goal never exited.\n   - One drill handed control to the pass receiver, so it couldn't be won.\n   - Online client copies hit a drawing error every frame.\n   - Mouse hover in menus read the wrong screen coordinates.\n3. **Most helpful:** the bot playing the whole Cup Run and all 7 drills through the real controls.\n4. **Least helpful:** the engine checker's \"one flat color\" note. Half of a hockey screen is meant to be ice.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** the exact fixed timestep and the headless `game.advance` with simulated button presses, which let a bot play the whole story in about 2 minutes. Second: being able to wrap the character rig's pose code.\n2. **Got stuck / guessed wrong:**\n   - The broadcast view draws at 1.5× at zoom 1, so that was far too close.\n   - `mouseScreen()` includes the camera offset rather than HUD pixels.\n   - Moving the mouse doesn't mark it as the device in use.\n   - Every zoom level redraws the whole floor.\n   - In the ice-level view, y grows toward the camera.\n3. **Most important thing I built:** the hockey simulation (skating momentum, the puck against curved boards, posts and nets, and a goalie who has to be physically in the way), then the gear and motion on the rig, and the online layer.\n4. **Hardest part:** drawing curved boards, glass and nets in a tilted view with correct depth.\n5. **Changes made (6, all logged in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`):**\n   - PlayStation symbols added to the pixel font.\n   - Options is ignored while Create is held, because AI Hub reserves that combination.\n   - Hockey sticks are accepted as weapons, and a hook lets the game draw its own gear inside the character.\n   - Organ and brass sounds, a filter, and a separate voice volume.\n   - Each speaker in a dialog box can babble in their own voice.\n   - 12 cached floor drawings instead of 6.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** make zooming cheap, so a broadcast camera can punch in and pull out smoothly.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** concrete deliverables, a pass/fail harness for online play, a strict one-command testing method, and a detailed plan.\n2. **Confusing:** the plan's file names (`max-…174816`) differ from TASK.md's (`xhigh-…182647`); I followed TASK.md as STAGE.md says. \"Multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own\" can be read two ways, so I built hub sessions plus local 1–4 players.\n3. **Hardest:** the scope (story, drills, local and online multiplayer, minigames, attract and dev modes) in one session, and judging game feel without playing it by hand.\n4. **Would change:** rank the features as must-have versus nice-to-have, and keep the plan's names in step with TASK.md."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":492,"cache_read_input_tokens":871433,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":492},"output_tokens":811,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2bfc1153-d85f-4cc7-a4e9-74837d1d317d","uuid":"885b520d-eead-4777-a91f-8440f47074f2","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:35:03.525Z","request_id":"req_011CghGKzUXXesyAAVm66rdR"}
