{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghHXEamMgo2aP9DeZw6C","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ice Hoc-Kaboom is finished. On the final build, `node tests/run.mjs` passed **255 checks with 0 failures** (7 headless suites and 3 in Chromium). The game is one file: `IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-182647.html`. The test run started after the last code change, and the game's own script has no TODO, FIXME, placeholder or \"not implemented\" text.\n\nChecking the features you flagged through real play paths turned up and fixed one real problem. A perfect Flood Wagon could not actually be earned after a real period: drivers only cleared 37–70% of the scuffed ice, because the wagon circled its target instead of reaching it. It now brakes into hard turns and lays a wider sheet, and clearing 85% of the scuff counts as perfect (a gold tick on the progress bar marks it).\n\nThe new feature checks:\n- **Golden Twister:** in a real CPU-vs-CPU game against the Grizzlies, Sterling chooses it himself and spins with the puck. Every Twister ends in a curling shot, and the goalie reacts 25% later to it than to the same shot without the spin. Checking Sterling mid-spin is always a big hit and adds +10 BOOM.\n- **Gilded Mode:** in play, the turbo meter stays full while held, the slapshot charges in half the time and fires a KABOOM shot, and checks hit twice as hard.\n- **Gilded Boost and Doris's organ:** the Boost makes the Grizzlies exactly 10% faster from the 3rd period. Doris's organ adds +30 BOOM and 15% speed when played late, and wears off after 20 s.\n- **The Vault:** the first KABOOM shot only staggers him; the second cracks him (smaller, with lower reflexes).\n- **Cheats:** stars unlock them as planned. Driven through the real menus (title → Exhibition → ready → Cheats set to \"All on\" → Face off), the match starts with all five on, and they change the game: big heads, retro rigs, permanent KABOOM, and octopi raining.\n- **Flood Wagon:** after a real period, a player mowing the ice gets a perfect flood and +30 BOOM, and the next period starts. A player who doesn't drive gets nothing.\n\nThe About pages show exactly the text in `exit-interview.json`, and the engine-changes page is now built from `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`. The exit interview parses and answers every field. Cost and token counts are \"unknown\" because this environment doesn't show them, which is what TASK.md asks for in that case.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:26:47\n- **Source:** Ice hockey (over-the-top arcade style)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (3-on-3 hockey with goalies), with a fight minigame, a Flood Wagon minigame and a five-chapter story (the Cup Run). A TV-broadcast camera at a three-quarter angle follows the puck, with an Overhead option and ice-level shots for fights, replays, intros, celebrations and cutscenes.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the handoff package, patched), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript in 41 modules bundled into one HTML file. Tests use Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 (Chromium).\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-era pixel art and HD character rigs. New hockey gear, rinks with curved boards, breakable glass and nets, team logos, five dressed arenas with crowds, the Flood Wagon and the Cup, all drawn in code.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural rigs, with new hockey motion layered on top: skating, stops, crossovers, stick-handling, every pass and shot, checks, dekes, goalie stances and saves, faceoffs and celebrations. Fights use the engine's punch moves, and there is an animation lab.\n- **Audio:** All in code: 11 songs plus 6 goal stings, about 45 sound effects, a goal horn, organ stings, and a crowd that reacts and chants. Voices are Animalese-style babble.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 1–4 players, where the host runs the game and the other copies predict their own skater. There is also 1–4 player local play.\n- **Testing:**\n  - One command runs everything.\n  - Headless suites: systems; features (boss rules, unlocks, Flood Wagon); text; dev mode; attract mode; a bot playing the whole Cup Run and all 7 drills; balance.\n  - Browser suites: the engine checker on 7 pages; menus with keyboard, mouse and touch-only; the AI Hub harness in 4 setups.\n  - I reviewed screenshots for the visuals.\n- **Research:** The engine's handoff docs and the AI Hub protocol and example. No web research on hockey.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** Cost unknown; tokens unknown (no figures available here); session time about 3 h 30 min (18:26 to about 21:55).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **Issues caught:** about 25 real ones, plus some bugs in the tests themselves. The final run is 255 passing and 0 failing.\n2. **Critical ones (7):**\n   - The CPU never went after a puck carrier standing by his own net.\n   - Drills without a puck looped forever.\n   - Drills finished by a goal never exited.\n   - One drill handed control to the pass receiver, so it couldn't be won.\n   - Online client copies hit a drawing error every frame.\n   - Mouse hover in menus read the wrong screen coordinates.\n   - A perfect Flood Wagon couldn't be earned after a real period.\n3. **Most helpful:** the bot playing the whole Cup Run and all 7 drills through the real controls.\n4. **Least helpful:** the engine checker's \"one flat color\" note. Half of a hockey screen is meant to be ice.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** the exact fixed timestep and the headless `game.advance` with simulated button presses, which let a bot play the whole story in about 2 minutes. Second: being able to wrap the character rig's pose code.\n2. **Got stuck / guessed wrong:**\n   - The broadcast view draws at 1.5× at zoom 1, so that was far too close.\n   - `mouseScreen()` includes the camera offset rather than HUD pixels.\n   - Moving the mouse doesn't mark it as the device in use.\n   - Every zoom level redraws the whole floor.\n   - In the ice-level view, y grows toward the camera.\n3. **Most important thing I built:** the hockey simulation (skating momentum, the puck against curved boards, posts and nets, and a goalie who has to be physically in the way), then the gear and motion on the rig, and the online layer.\n4. **Hardest part:** drawing curved boards, glass and nets in a tilted view with correct depth.\n5. **Changes made (6, all logged in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`):**\n   - PlayStation symbols added to the pixel font.\n   - Options is ignored while Create is held, because AI Hub reserves that combination.\n   - Hockey sticks are accepted as weapons, and a hook lets the game draw its own gear inside the character.\n   - Organ and brass sounds, a filter, and a separate voice volume.\n   - Each speaker in a dialog box can babble in their own voice.\n   - 12 cached floor drawings instead of 6.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** make zooming cheap, so a broadcast camera can punch in and pull out smoothly.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** concrete deliverables, a pass/fail harness for online play, a strict one-command testing method, and a detailed plan.\n2. **Confusing:** the plan's file names (`max-…174816`) differ from TASK.md's (`xhigh-…182647`); I followed TASK.md as STAGE.md says. \"Multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own\" can be read two ways, so I built hub sessions plus local 1–4 players.\n3. **Hardest:** the scope (story, drills, local and online multiplayer, minigames, attract and dev modes) in one session, and judging game feel without playing it by hand.\n4. **Would change:** rank the features as must-have versus nice-to-have, and keep the plan's names in step with TASK.md."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1170,"cache_read_input_tokens":894904,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1170},"output_tokens":765,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2bfc1153-d85f-4cc7-a4e9-74837d1d317d","uuid":"b5a785ae-0bf6-4630-8c7f-866276edaa58","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:50:39.920Z","request_id":"req_011CghHXECwwCtH2mndtQCQx"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not all conditions are evidenced. The single HTML exists in the folder root, exit-interview.json was written and parses with every field answered, and the final reply contains all four sections. However, TASK.md requires every feature implemented with no placeholders or stubs, and the transcript does not fully establish this. Several TASK.md items were only partly verified: the Exhibition 'cheats' unlock was checked only at the setup config level in the earlier run and then via menus in the final feature suite, the Gilded Mode checks were loose, and the 'Hoser Hat Trick' and several TASK.md Records/Practice items were not individually exercised. Also, the usage figures are 'unknown' rather than measured, and the feature-by-feature mapping in the final reply is a self-report rather than a line-by-line audit of TASK.md against the code."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2bfc1153-d85f-4cc7-a4e9-74837d1d317d","uuid":"218d996a-9802-44f4-8c31-d1be6306d1c3","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:51:05.675Z","isSynthetic":true}
